On 26.10.2014 10:00, Jorgen Grahn wrote:
> 
> [...]  But on the other hand, I don't understand why both
> you and the wiki rank Wiz as hard and Mon as rather easy.

Well, not I but my algorihm based on the NAO data did that. :-)

But, frankly, I was astonished, too. I have not spent any time
for an analysis, though a few thoughts from former discussions
came to my mind. It had been said in the past that Wizards are
a preferred class, specifically for beginners. I tried to rule
those out by the preconditions of the chosen player/games set.
Also note that Wizards are not generated as dwarfs, the overall
most easy race to play. This may shift the success rate a bit.
In the class "A" Wizards are at rank 6, while in all the other
ascension classes (B-D) they rank at the worst places 12 resp.
13; this rough discrepancy between classes is indeed irritating.
Maybe someone can explain that, it must be something obvious
that I'm yet missing. (My own NAO Wizards are at rank 8.)

WRT Monk's difficulty I'm more confident. Through all classes
(A-D) they rank constantly at place 7-8, and the Wiki method
ranks them as 6; close enough. Personally I think they are not
too hard (my own monks together with priests are at rank 6-7).
With monks, while you advance levels you gain intrinsics, most
important poison resistance as early as level 3. Poison is
(after being killed while helpless) the second most death reason
in Nethack.

Another role ranking that I was a bit astonished of where the
Tourists; in the 'overall' column they rank better than Arc,
Wiz, and Rog. (My own Tou rank together with Arc at 12./13.)
The 'overall' Tourist result is clearly the effect of good
rankings in the C and D ascension classes (9. resp. 6.), but
why they are that good for casual ascenders is beyond me.
Maybe the "general purpose" character of that role is the
dominating factor? After all, the largest problem with all
classes is IMO to survive the very early game; most classes
(but the three champion classes) have that problem. Once you
are beyond that stage the "general purpose" advantages may
become significant.

(Just some ad hoc thoughts.)

As the Wiki says: "NetHack players widely disagree about which
roles are easiest or hardest." - I don't think it's that bad;
the overall difficulty of classes seems to be consensus, and
slight variations are matter of subjective experience and of
the perspective taken, the model the analysis is based upon.

